      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Subsea Cable Infrastructure, ADC/SJC2 Redundancy & Power Grid Resilience
      </h2>
      <p>
        In years past, Vietnam's international connectivity suffered from recurring physical breaks along the aging AAG (Asia-America Gateway) subsea cable system. However, the telecommunications landscape has experienced massive infrastructure capital investment:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>ADC (Asia Direct Cable)</strong>: Connects Vietnam directly to Hong Kong, Japan, Singapore, and the Philippines with high-capacity optical fiber pairs exceeding 140 Tbps aggregate design capacity.</li>
        <li><strong>SJC2 (South East Asia-Japan Cable 2)</strong>: Provides 126 Tbps of dedicated low-latency bandwidth, bypassing bottleneck chokepoints in the Luzon Strait.</li>
        <li><strong>Terrestrial Cross-Border Links</strong>: Vietnam's landline fiber backbone connects directly north into mainland China and west through Cambodia and Thailand, ensuring automated BGP failover within 120 milliseconds of an offshore fiber incident.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        On the municipal electricity side, Da Nang's Central Power Corporation operates a modern undergrounded medium-voltage distribution network along coastal tourist sectors. Rolling blackouts common to rural provinces do not occur in Ngu Hanh Son or Hai Chau. Furthermore, commercial coworking facilities and upscale serviced apartments maintain industrial automatic diesel backup generators capable of handling 100% HVAC and server loads during routine municipal maintenance.
      </p>

